---
/*
 * "How to read this data": what the measures mean, how the comparisons and
 * trends are worked out, what the dataset leaves out, and the sources. Same
 * on the browse page and every school page.
 */
import { SIMILAR_PTS } from '../../lib/schools/values.js';
import { DATASET_URL, DATASET_NAME, DATASET_ID, LOCATION_SOURCES, LINKS, TARGET_BANDS, REPORTS, yearLabel } from '../../lib/schools/config.js';
import { metricsFor } from '../../lib/schools/metrics.js';
import { date } from '../../lib/format.js';

interface Props {
  meta: { rowsUpdatedAt: string | null } | null;
  latestYear?: number | null;
}
const { meta, latestYear } = Astro.props;
const locations = Object.entries(LOCATION_SOURCES);
const mostMeasures = Math.max(...Object.keys(REPORTS).map((r) => metricsFor(r).length));
---

<section class="dash-section sq-about" id="about-this-data" aria-labelledby="sq-about-title">
  <h2 class="chart-title" id="sq-about-title">How to read this data</h2>

  <details class="about-data">
    <summary>What the numbers mean</summary>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p>
        <strong>School types.</strong> Each school gets a School Quality Report from the NYC Department of Education
        (DOE) for each kind of school it is: elementary and middle (including K–8), high school, transfer high school,
        early childhood (K–3), District 75 (citywide special education) or Young Adult Borough Center (evening
        programs, which stopped getting reports after 2018–19). A 6–12 school has two reports, and its page shows both.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Which measures.</strong> The reports have over 100 measures per school type, many of them broken out
        by student group. We show up to {mostMeasures} per type, fewer for some: test results or graduation and college outcomes, attendance, and
        how former students did at their next school. Everything else is in the dataset.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>DOE target ratings.</strong> For many measures DOE scores the school from 1.00 to 4.99 against targets
        set from similar schools, and we show the band:
        {TARGET_BANDS.map((b, i) => <>{i ? ', ' : ' '}<span class="sq-band" data-band={b.tone}>{b.label}</span> ({b.min.toFixed(2)}{i ? `–${(b.min + 0.99).toFixed(2)}` : '+'})</>)}.
        A measure without a band isn’t one DOE scores, or wasn’t scored that year.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>“Not reported”</strong> means the dataset has no value: too few students were counted for DOE to
        publish it, or the measure didn’t apply that year. We never treat it as zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Students</strong> is the number of students with attendance records, a close stand-in for enrollment.
        It’s context, not a sign of quality.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="about-data">
    <summary>How we compare and spot trends</summary>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p>
        <strong>NYC average</strong> is our calculation: the average of every school of the same type that year
        (elementary, middle and K–8 schools each get their own), weighted by how many students each counted. For a rate
        like graduation, that’s the citywide rate for that kind of school. <strong>Similar schools</strong> is DOE’s comparison group average for this school, from the
        dataset; DOE didn’t publish it every year.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Better, about the same or worse.</strong> Each measure is better or worse than the NYC average when it’s
        more than {SIMILAR_PTS} percentage points away in the direction that’s better for students, and about the same
        otherwise. For chronic absenteeism, lower is better.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Trend</strong> uses the last 3 school years with a value. We fit a straight line through them and call it
        improving or declining when it moved at least 3 percentage points over those years (1 point for average
        attendance, 2 for changes in attendance, moves to less restrictive settings and moves out of District 75), and steady otherwise.
        “Improving” means better for students: for chronic absenteeism, that’s going down.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Gaps.</strong> New York cancelled state tests in spring 2020 (COVID-19) and made them optional in spring
        2021, so test results for 2019–20 and 2020–21 are blank. Charts shade missing years instead of drawing a line
        across them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>New tests.</strong> New York gave new grades 3–8 ELA and math tests, with new score cutoffs, starting in
        spring 2023. Proficiency from 2022–23 on can’t be compared with earlier years, so trends start there and the
        charts mark the change. College and career readiness has been reported in its current form only since 2023–24.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="about-data">
    <summary>What isn’t here</summary>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p>
        This dataset has the report’s measures but not DOE’s <strong>framework ratings</strong> (Rigorous Instruction,
        Collaborative Teachers, Supportive Environment, Effective School Leadership, Strong Family-Community Ties,
        Trust, Student Achievement), <strong>Quality Review</strong> ratings, enrollment or the <strong>Economic Need
        Index</strong>. NYC Open Data has those only as single-year files from 2019–20 and earlier, so we leave them
        out rather than show old ratings as current. Each school’s full report, with those ratings, is on
        <a href={LINKS.sqrHome}>DOE’s School Quality Reports page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Addresses</strong> come from DOE’s 2019–20 school locations, with ZIP codes from the city’s Facilities
        Database. Schools that opened since are matched by name; a few still have no address and show “Address not
        available.” A school may have moved since 2019–20.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <footer class="section-sources">
    <h3 class="sources-title">Where this comes from</h3>
    <p>
      <a href={DATASET_URL}>{DATASET_NAME}</a> on NYC Open Data (dataset {DATASET_ID}), published by the NYC Department of
      Education.{meta?.rowsUpdatedAt && <> Last updated {date(meta.rowsUpdatedAt)}.</>}
      {latestYear && <> The newest school year in it is {yearLabel(latestYear)}.</>} We check for new data daily.
    </p>
    <p>
      Measures, target ratings and similar-school averages come from NYC DOE School Quality Reports. NYC averages and
      trends are our calculations. Addresses: {locations.map(([id, s], i) => <>{i ? ' and ' : ''}<a href={s.url}>{s.name}</a> ({id})</>)}.
    </p>
    <ul class="caveats">
      <li>One year’s numbers describe one group of students. Small schools can swing a lot from year to year.</li>
      <li>Test scores and graduation rates reflect who a school serves as well as how well it teaches. Compare with similar schools, and visit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={DATASET_URL}>Report a data problem</a> to NYC Open Data.</p>
  </footer>
</section>
